{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויקחו אליך שמן זית", :translit "vi'yik'hu eilekha shemen zayit", :gloss "and they shall take to you olive oil"} {:he "שמן זית", :translit "shemen zayit", :gloss "olive oil"} {:he "סוכה", :translit "sukkah", :gloss "Sukkah"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut ha-parashiyot: the phrase 'shemen zayit' at the end of this parashah is juxtaposed to the opening of Parashat ha-Mo'adot beginning with 'Sukkot,' linking the two passages."}], :frame {:text "The Talmud establishes that Hallel is recited all eight days of Hanukkah and asks on what basis, since Hanukkah is rabbinic and Hallel is typically biblical. The answer given is that Hanukkah is compared to Sukkot, which is eight days and has a full Hallel (Sukkah 28a–b; Arakhin 10a). Ba'al HaTurim locates the textual basis for this comparison in the juxtaposition of 'olive oil' to the festival of Sukkah in the verse sequence, since the preceding parashah ends with the blasphemer narrative and the next begins the festival calendar with Sukkot.", :sources ["Sukkah 28a–b" "Arakhin 10a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "להעלות נר", :translit "leha'alot ner", :gloss "to raise up a lamp"} {:he "נר", :translit "ner", :gloss "lamp (singular)"} {:he "נרות", :translit "nerot", :gloss "lamps (plural)"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Shinui lashon: the verse shifts from singular 'ner' to plural 'nerot' in the same passage, signaling a progression from one lamp to many."}], :frame {:text "The Talmud records the dispute between Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel regarding the order of kindling Hanukkah lights. Beit Shammai reduce the number each night while Beit Hillel add, and the halakhah follows Beit Hillel that one kindles one on the first night and adds each subsequent night (Shabbat 21b). Ba'al HaTurim reads this progression of increasing light into the verse's own movement from the singular 'lamp' to the plural 'lamps.'", :sources ["Shabbat 21b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut ha-parashiyot: the phrase 'shemen zayit' at the end of this parashah is juxtaposed to the opening of Parashat ha-Mo'adot beginning with 'Sukkot,' linking the two passages.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Shinui lashon: the verse shifts from singular 'ner' to plural 'nerot' in the same passage, signaling a progression from one lamp to many.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:25:17.765836467Z", :model "glm-5.2"}